{% section-card tone="intro" %}
## What is ambient computing architecture?

**Ambient computing architecture** coordinates sensors, context models, policies, and
actuators so a digital service can respond to a person's environment without requiring
a traditional screen for every interaction. A room may adjust lighting when occupancy
is sufficiently clear, while a doorway should still require explicit user authority.

It matters because sensing is probabilistic and physical actions have consequences. The
core invariant is: **context confidence may justify a recommendation, but it never
grants more authority than the behavior's policy allows.** Every important automation
also needs a visible state, a manual path, and a bounded fallback when evidence or
connectivity disappears.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="cyan" %}
## Treat ambient behavior as a controlled decision loop

{% concept-grid columns="4" %}
{% concept-card title="Sense for a purpose" eyebrow="Evidence" icon="search" tone="cyan" %}
Collect only signals that support a declared behavior. Record source, freshness,
quality, and consent instead of treating every reading as equally trustworthy.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Infer with uncertainty" eyebrow="Context" icon="activity" tone="violet" %}
Fuse evidence into a bounded context claim. Preserve the score and contributing
signals so the system can explain why it reached that claim.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Authorize separately" eyebrow="Policy" icon="shield" tone="amber" %}
Limit automatic execution by action impact, reversibility, user preference, and current
consent. A high score does not override this ceiling.
{% /concept-card %}
{% concept-card title="Act and expose state" eyebrow="Control" icon="repeat" tone="green" %}
Publish the action, offer undo where possible, and keep a physical or explicit manual
control available when the ambient path fails.
{% /concept-card %}
{% /concept-grid %}

{% system-flow title="Ambient decision path" caption="Evidence and authority are independent gates. The audit event lets users and operators reconstruct the action." %}
{% system-node title="Purpose-limited signals" eyebrow="Sense" icon="search" tone="cyan" %}
Read fresh device events with source and quality metadata.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Context claim" eyebrow="Infer" icon="activity" tone="violet" %}
Fuse evidence and preserve uncertainty.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Authority policy" eyebrow="Authorize" icon="shield" tone="amber" %}
Cap what this behavior may do automatically.
{% /system-node %}
{% system-node title="Actuator and audit" eyebrow="Act" icon="repeat" tone="green" %}
Execute, expose state, and retain a bounded event.
{% /system-node %}
{% /system-flow %}
{% /section-card %}

{% interactive-block id="fundamentals/ambient-computing-architecture-calculator" dataFile="/api/content/fundamentals/ambient-computing-architecture/data/context-decision-model.json" /%}

{% section-card accent="amber" %}
## Separate evidence thresholds from authority ceilings

- **Evidence quality** answers whether the context claim is sufficiently supported.
  Discount stale signals, detect correlated sources, and expose missing evidence.
- **Action authority** answers what may happen next. Suggestion, confirmation, and
  automatic execution are policy choices, not confidence bands.
- **Impact and reversibility** determine the ceiling. Lighting can be undone; physical
  access, payments, health actions, and safety controls require stricter boundaries.
- **User agency** requires a legible current state, correction path, pause control, and
  a record of important actions.

{% callout variant="warn" %}
Adding more sensors can increase surveillance and correlated failure without improving
the decision. Measure whether each signal changes a defined outcome before collecting it.
{% /callout %}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Place data and decisions deliberately

Local, gateway, and cloud processing are deployment choices. They change latency,
offline behavior, raw-data exposure, update cadence, and operational ownership.

{% process-flow %}
{% process-step number="1" title="Declare the behavior" label="Purpose" %}
Name the user outcome, allowed action, evidence, owner, and non-goals before selecting
sensors or models.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="2" title="Minimize the boundary" label="Data" %}
Keep raw signals close to their source when a local claim or compact event is enough.
Assign retention to each boundary rather than to the product as a whole.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="3" title="Design degraded behavior" label="Resilience" %}
Define stale-state limits, local rules, manual control, and recovery reconciliation for
sensor, gateway, network, and cloud failures.
{% /process-step %}
{% process-step number="4" title="Verify lived outcomes" label="Operate" %}
Measure false actions, missed actions, overrides, data flows, and fallback use. Revisit
the policy when the environment or population changes.
{% /process-step %}
{% /process-flow %}
{% /section-card %}

{% interactive-block id="fundamentals/ambient-computing-architecture-resilience-lab" dataFile="/api/content/fundamentals/ambient-computing-architecture/data/privacy-resilience-model.json" /%}

{% section-card accent="violet" %}
## Make policy executable and reviewable

Keep thresholds, authority, freshness, placement, retention, and fallback behavior in
versioned configuration. The decision function should return a reason, not only a
boolean, so product UX and audit systems can expose the same outcome.

{% code-block file="/api/content/fundamentals/ambient-computing-architecture/code/evaluate-context-policy.ts" language="typescript" title="Evaluate evidence and authority as separate gates" /%}

{% code-block file="/api/content/fundamentals/ambient-computing-architecture/code/ambient-boundary-policy.yaml" language="yaml" title="Declare purpose, data placement, and degraded behavior" /%}
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="rose" %}
## Test failure and misuse before deployment

- replay stale or reordered sensor events and confirm they cannot silently trigger a
  fresh-context action;
- remove one signal at a time and measure whether uncertainty and UX change honestly;
- disconnect device, gateway, and cloud paths independently and verify the documented
  local or manual fallback;
- test identity changes, shared spaces, guests, children, and revoked consent rather
  than assuming one permanent user;
- verify physical safety interlocks remain outside probabilistic automation;
- inspect logs and exports to ensure raw context does not cross undeclared boundaries;
- measure overrides and complaints as product signals, not user error.
{% /section-card %}

{% section-card accent="blue" %}
## Primary references

- [W3C Web of Things Architecture 1.1](https://www.w3.org/TR/wot-architecture/) defines
  interoperable Thing, Consumer, intermediary, security, and privacy boundaries.
- [W3C Web of Things Profiles](https://www.w3.org/TR/wot-profile/) narrows flexible Thing
  Descriptions into implementable interoperability profiles.
- [NIST Privacy Framework](https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework) provides outcomes for
  identifying, governing, controlling, communicating, and protecting privacy risk.
{% /section-card %}

